### Why the ponsonbydoctors DMARC record gets replaced

The record currently reads `v=DMARC1; p=none; rua=mailto:rua@dmarc.brevo.com` — published by Brevo when the domain was onboarded. It is **valid and working**; Brevo's external-destination authorisation is confirmed present (`ponsonbydoctors.co.nz._report._dmarc.dmarc.brevo.com` returns `v=DMARC1`), so they are entitled to receive the reports and their address should stay.

Two things it lacks, both of which the other four domains have:

- **The aggregate reports go only to Brevo.** They describe every source sending as `@ponsonbydoctors.co.nz` — including Google Workspace, which Brevo has no interest in. Without a copy, there is no way to confirm the domain is clean before tightening to `p=quarantine`.
- **No `fo=1`**, so no forensic failure reports.

Adding your own address alongside Brevo's needs **no** extra authorisation record, because it is on the same domain as the DMARC record itself. Both destinations receive a copy.

### Mailboxes to create

Every `rua` address has to be deliverable or the reports bounce and `p=none` buys nothing. In Google Workspace the cheapest option is an **alias on an existing mailbox**, or a **Group** if more than one person should see them. One per domain:

| Address | Needed for | Exists? |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `dmarc@luxradiology.co.nz` | already published in DMARC | ☐ confirm |
| `dmarc@ponsonbydoctors.co.nz` | required before the record above goes in | ☐ confirm |
| `dmarc@sygnal.com` | already published in DMARC | ☐ confirm |
| `dmarc@lapelle.co.nz` | already published in DMARC | ☐ confirm |
| `dmarc@sonusear.co.nz` | required before its DMARC record goes in | ☐ confirm |

> [!NOTE]
> A missing `rua` mailbox does **not** break mail delivery or the Outlook fix — it only costs you the reports. Nothing here is urgent, but it is what makes the eventual move to `p=quarantine` safe rather than a guess.

## Per-domain notes

**ponsonbydoctors.co.nz** — ✅ Done. Sends through **both** Google Workspace and Brevo; Brevo's DKIM (`brevo1` / `brevo2` CNAMEs) was already wired up and Brevo published the DMARC record. SPF and the Google DKIM selector are now in, and a test message verifies `dkim=pass header.i=@ponsonbydoctors.co.nz header.s=google` with `spf=pass` and `dmarc=pass`. One loose end: `rua` still points only at Brevo's collector, so the aggregate reports go to Brevo rather than to you. To see them yourself: `v=DMARC1; p=none; rua=mailto:dmarc@ponsonbydoctors.co.nz,mailto:rua@dmarc.brevo.com; fo=1`.

**sygnal.com** — DNS complete, signing **not yet confirmed**. All three records are published and correct, but no inbound test message exists to prove Google is signing with the new selector. A self-addressed test will not work: `mike@` → `mike@` is internal to the tenant, never crosses SMTP, and gets no meaningful `Authentication-Results`. Either send to an address outside Google Workspace and read the headers there, or simply wait for the first DMARC aggregate report at `dmarc@sygnal.com` (24–48h), which states the SPF and DKIM result for every sending source. Still worth confirming nothing other than Google Workspace sends as `@sygnal.com` — anything missed is now softfailing.

**sonusear.co.nz** — ⚠️ **Outstanding.** Still no SPF and no DMARC; the Google DKIM record is published (valid RSA-2048) but with nothing else in place it authenticates nothing for DMARC purposes. This is the only domain still exposed to the Outlook rejections. Both records from the table above need to go in, then a test message to confirm the DKIM signs with `s=google` rather than a `gappssmtp.com` fallback.

**lapelle.co.nz** — ✅ Done. SPF was already correct with a hard `-all`; the Google DKIM selector and DMARC are now in. Verified on a test message: `dkim=pass header.i=@lapelle.co.nz header.s=google`, `spf=pass`, `dmarc=pass`. The DKIM is what makes that `-all` safe on forwarded mail.

## How to verify each one

Run against the authoritative nameserver, then confirm on a live message. All read-only.

```bash
dig +short @ram.ns.cloudflare.com <domain> TXT | grep spf1
dig +short @ram.ns.cloudflare.com _dmarc.<domain> TXT
dig +short @ram.ns.cloudflare.com google._domainkey.<domain> TXT
```

DNS alone cannot prove DKIM is live — publishing the record and clicking **Start authentication** in Google Admin are separate actions. The only proof is the `Authentication-Results` header on a message sent *after* the change:

```
dkim=pass   header.i=@<domain>  header.s=google
spf=pass    smtp.mailfrom=<user>@<domain>
dmarc=pass  header.from=<domain>
```

If `header.i` still reads `…gappssmtp.com`, the record is published but Google has not switched over yet.

## Working order per domain

1. Publish SPF and DMARC in Cloudflare — two minutes, and DMARC alone stops the Outlook rejections.
2. Generate the DKIM record in Google Admin, publish it, then click **Start authentication**.
3. Send a test message to a Gmail address and read the headers for the three passes above.
4. Send a test to the affected outlook.com recipient to close the loop. Microsoft may cache the old negative DMARC lookup for up to an hour — wait and retry before assuming a fault.
5. After a few weeks of clean aggregate reports, tighten `p=none` → `p=quarantine`, and `~all` → `-all`.

